{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 32, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born in 1140 as the only son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His early years were marked by the political turbulence of the Crusader states. Raymond inherited the county at the age of twelve after his father was assassinated in 1152. Because of his young age a regency was established under his mother and later under his uncle Baldwin III of Jerusalem. During this time Tripoli was a vital but vulnerable Christian stronghold on the Levantine coast. Raymond’s early rule was shaped by the constant threat of Muslim forces led by Nur ad-Din and later Saladin.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III became a central figure in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He married Eschiva of Bures in 1158 and later became the guardian of the young King Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. Raymond was appointed regent of Jerusalem in 1174 after the death of Amalric I. His regency was troubled by internal divisions among the nobility and the growing power of the military orders. Raymond often clashed with Guy of Lusignan who was married to Sibylla the king’s sister. Raymond opposed Guy’s rise to power and was eventually removed from the regency in 1176. He retreated to Tripoli but remained an influential voice in the court. His rivalry with Guy and the Templars contributed to the weakening of Christian unity before the disastrous Battle of Hattin.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III played a key role in the events leading to the fall of Jerusalem. He was one of the few nobles who advocated for peace with Saladin and opposed reckless military campaigns. He was present at the Battle of Hattin in 1187 where the Crusader army was crushed. Raymond escaped the battlefield and returned to Tripoli. He died later that year in 1187 possibly from illness or the stress of defeat. His death marked the end of a long and complex reign that reflected the struggles of the Crusader states to survive amid internal strife and external pressure. Raymond was remembered as a cautious and pragmatic leader who tried to preserve his realm despite overwhelming odds. His legacy is tied to the decline of Christian power in the Holy Land.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -4.625213477993384e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.002193308901041746}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -2.288792165927589e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.005234109237790108}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -1.0251946150674485e-05}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.005193670280277729}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.00890072900801897}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.10172396153211594}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -7.986990567587782e-06}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0012731788447126746}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00032228996860794723}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -5.4596363042946905e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.4305104514278355e-06}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.38688924908638}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.0019911956042051315}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -2.3841830625315197e-06}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -9.775113539944869e-06}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -2.0265558760002023e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -3.6954811548639555e-06}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.00